Before this commit, every internal/engine.Run process pinned clientCfg.ListenPort to config.PortRangeStart unconditionally. That's not a crash or a returned error anywhere -- torrent.Client just silently continues without an inbound listener bound on that port for every torrent after the first, so those torrents fall back to outbound-only peer connections. Nothing in list, state.json, or the logs distinguished that degraded state from a healthy one, and it quietly defeated any router port-forwarding the user had configured for the range -- a real correctness bug with zero visible symptom.

Run now calls portpool.Claim(id, *globalCfg) before constructing the torrent.Client, and only sets clientCfg.ListenPort = claimedPort when a port was actually claimed (claimedPort > 0); an exhausted range instead gets ephemeral=true and a logged warning, falling back to an OS-assigned port rather than failing to start.

The release-timing detail that isn't obvious from a diff skim: the defer for portpool.Release(id) is registered immediately once the claim succeeds -- before torrent.NewClient, AddTorrent, or metadata resolution run -- not merely alongside the pre-existing graceful-shutdown paths further down (SIGTERM/pause, normal completion). An early failure in any of those later steps must still release the claimed port, or it leaks from the registry for the lifetime of the daemon-less coordination scheme. The defer is also only registered in the claimedPort > 0 branch, so a torrent that never claimed anything (no range configured, or the ephemeral fallback) never touches the registry/lock file trying to release a claim it never made.
